# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: benchmark-autocad-windows-hangs-and-blocks-release — a silent 2-hour job sits in front of every release stage
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Context

Observed while cutting =release-1.8.49=. The tag pipeline reached the
=benchmark= stage, and =benchmark:autocad:windows= then held the Windows
runner — which has concurrency 1 — for two hours, until the project job
timeout killed it. Only then did =release:windows:x86-64= start.

The same job stalled =release-1.8.47=; it was cancelled by hand that time.
Twice now it has been the single thing standing between a green tag and its
artefacts.

* Problem, part 1: it hangs

The job has never succeeded. Its siblings on the same machine finish in
about a minute and a half:

| job                        | duration |
|----------------------------+----------|
| =benchmark:bricscad:windows=  | 1m37     |
| =benchmark:clautolisp:windows= | 1m16     |
| =benchmark:bricscad:macos=     | 1m00     |
| =benchmark:autocad:windows=    | killed at the 120 min timeout |

This is consistent with the known accoreconsole failure mode (see
=accoreconsole-deported-load-highbyte.issue= and the stranded-process
incident that killed =release-1.8.41=): AutoCAD starts and never returns.

* Problem, part 2: the hang is invisible

Even granting that it hangs, nothing in the job log says so. The script is

#+begin_src yaml
- make -C autolisp-benchmark run-autocad-windows *>&1 | Out-File -Encoding utf8 dist/bench/autocad-windows.txt
- Get-Content dist/bench/autocad-windows.txt
#+end_src

Every byte of output goes into a file, and the file is only printed by the
*next* step. So while the benchmark runs, the trace shows the =make= command
and then nothing at all — and if the job never finishes, the =Get-Content=
never runs and the output is *never* shown. A job that hangs produces
exactly the same trace as a job that is working: the log ends at the same
line either way. There is no way to tell them apart from outside.

That is the more serious half. The hang costs two hours; the silence is
what makes an operator sit and wait through them, unable to decide.

* Why it matters for releases

=benchmark= is a stage in front of =release=, so this job gates the release
lanes even though its results are not release products. =allow_failure: true=
saves the pipeline — the release stage does start once the job dies — but
not the two hours.

* Options

1. Give the CAD benchmark jobs a short =timeout:= of their own (5–10 min).
   A benchmark whose siblings take 90 seconds does not need 120 minutes, and
   this alone caps the damage without deciding anything about AutoCAD.
2. =tee= instead of =Out-File= so the trace shows progress live, the way
   =benchmark:bricscad:macos= already does with =2>&1 | tee=. The Windows
   equivalent is =| Tee-Object=. This makes a hang diagnosable.
3. Move =benchmark= after =collect=, or off the release-tag path entirely,
   so no benchmark can ever gate an artefact.
4. Fix or disable the AutoCAD benchmark itself — the underlying accoreconsole
   problem is tracked separately.

Options 1 and 2 are independent of the AutoCAD question and worth doing on
their own: together they turn a two-hour silent block into a ten-minute
failure with a readable log.
